Twelve hosts, eleven decoys. The one you want has something
unusual that none of the others have. Read top to bottom.

If you're still stuck:
  - Look at the lines that start with something other than "PORT"
    or "Not shown" or "Host is up". Those are extra notes nmap
    only emits for hosts that have something special.
  - One host has TWO names — its forward DNS says one thing, its
    rDNS says something else. That contradiction is the clue.

The flag is HOST:PORT — the address an attacker would actually
connect to from outside. Not the internal address where the
service really lives (that's behind the gateway and only has ssh
exposed directly).
